we're launching a new series called  rebuilding america to showcase the  ingenuity of individuals and communities  who are working together to build a  better world one project at a time this  morning we head to new mexico to the  biggest cluster of out-of-this-world  homes known as earthships they're at the  forefront of sustainable housing and  built from things you might find in a  junkyard janet chamblin shows us how  concerns about climate change and energy  prices are driving a huge increase in  their popularity  along a rural road in northern new  mexico they rise from the dusty desert  with futuristic shapes resembling  something from another planet but these  are what's known as earth ships off the  grid homes built into the ground made  mostly from recycled materials what some  would call garbage  like used tires tightly packed with dirt  for insulation and structural walls  and old bottles that become part of the  interior it's kind of like stained glass  but we're using all recycled bottles  there are no power lines water lines or  sewage lines connected to this earth  ship under construction  electricity is generated from solar  panels water is collected on the roof  then filtered and the treated sewage  nourishes plants and trees this is the  outer greenhouse 76 year old architect  michael reynolds is the grandfather of  the earthship concept so this is kind of  the inner part of the building building  the first one in 1979 and teaching  others ever since  there are more than 100 earth ships here  potentially many more soon are you able  to quantify the increase in you know  inquiries that you're getting well i  would say it's doubled  in the last couple years uh no in in the  last couple of months  skyrocketing energy prices and a growing  desire to live sustainably amid climate  change are fueling demand for homes that  generally don't use fuel what's it like  to live in an earthship  uh  it was a very very small leap the what  you're told at the beginning oh my  goodness you're off grid that's got to  be the worst thing in the world john  lasalla spent 40 years living and  working in new york city i love the way  this house functions he bought this air  ship five years ago for about 300  thousand dollars and says he hasn't  looked back you practice what we all  should be practicing which is  conservation so if there's clouds for  three days you don't turn on the  inverter which means you have  electricity for the primary stuff but  your computer and your stereo can't plug  in this room  was uh built by the original owners his  1200 square foot home has no microwave  and no clothing dryer on the other hand  the propane to heat water cost him about  90 dollars a year  no regrets no none at all not at all i  wouldn't live any other way  a way others are considering we have a  few things going on today to finish the  house this is the earthship academy  a four-week immersive class where people  from all over travel to the community  about a 30-minute drive from taos  learning like this hollywood production  designer bianca faro how to build an  earthship every job we build all our  sets and then we throw everything in the  trash and so i've just found over the  years that in my free time  i just want to combat that in some way i  want to find solutions this home is  already sold to a florida couple  the students live in an earthship while  they learn you just have to come and see  and experience it i've been living in  one for almost a month you don't need a  class to check out life off the grid so  this is just a master bedroom with a  walk-in closet down below homes like  this are available on vacation rental  sites it's a chance to test drive a  lifestyle of extreme sustainability  just don't bring a hair dryer what would  you say to someone who's looking around  and interested in  potentially living this way your typical  human still  lives for the american dream my custom  home with my dressing room that has room  for 60 pairs of shoes and blah blah blah  blah blah those days in my mind i'm  already seeing it  are gone earth ships are an oddity  but amid renewed interest in living  untethered a surge of eco-friendly  construction here  and for those who've already made the  lifestyle choice validation it's kind of  like a conspiracy of enlightenment you  know everybody's their way but they live  here all for basically the same reason  they want to be off grid they like the  isolation of it they like the stunning  beauty of it and they think this is a  better way to go other worldly homes  designed to protect earth for cbs  morning's janet chamleon at the  earthship community in new mexico  i am fascinated by this yeah yeah it's  very cool looking but i would like some  neighbors  i don't want to live out there i live in  new mexico that's where this was yeah  and i like new mexico but i would just  like some neighbors but i do think it's  very cool i like what he said a  conspiracy of enlightenment and that you  could test drive the lifestyle if you  want to to see if it's for you but the  house looks nice yeah i don't want to  generalize people in america but i'll  describe myself i do live in excess and  i realize that you know you walk around  your house you have more than you need  lights on everywhere yeah and you're  wasting energy and i think to myself as  i am an aspiring minimalist how can i  downsize my life in a way that is more  fulfilling but then also better for the  world and this right here is an  opportunity so are you ready to test  drive i'm going to test it out i'm going  to rent it i'm going to listen one of my  next vacations i'll rent it okay sure  i'll let you all right we're coming  right rebuilding america is sponsored by  steel find yours at steeleusa.com
 

